  abstract={The development of Industry 5.0 technology has promoted the increasing application of autonomous driving technology in Intelligent Transportation Systems (ITS), which has greatly reduced traffic accidents, but there is still the problem of insufficient vehicle stability under extreme conditions.Therefore, a terminal complementary sliding mode control (TCSMC) scheme using long-short term memory (LSTM) structure is proposed for the direct yaw-moment control (DYC) architecture used in four-wheel independently driven vehicles (FWIDV). LSTM can approximate uncertain nonlinear disturbances in the dynamics model more accurately, which can learn features at different levels of abstraction. In addition, the proposed TCSMC combines the complementary mechanism with the terminal sliding mode control to ensure that the controller does not depend on the precise dynamics model, thus improving the robustness and control accuracy of the controller. Subsequently, the external yaw moment was calculated from deviation of the yaw rate and sideslip angle; meanwhile, the optimal distribution strategy is chosen to distribute torque. Finally, simulation experiments demonstrate that the proposed control scheme significantly enhances robustness and reduces the output error of the controller. The proposed stability controller can effectively improve the reliability and safety of autonomous vehicles in extreme environments and extend the range of applications for autonomous driving.},
  keywords={Tires;Wheels;Long short term memory;Vehicle dynamics;Torque;Safety;Sliding mode control;Stability criteria;Accuracy;Mathematical models;Autonomous vehicles;terminal complementary sliding mode control;stability control;consumer-centric vehicles;long-short term memory},
